1
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:11,790
This video marks the start of

2
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:13,340
the Link Layer Module in

3
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:15,500
the Networking
Fundamentals course.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,560
To overview, what
we'll be covering in

5
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,700
this module will highlight

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,720
the main problems addressed
at the link layer.

7
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:29,040
To start, let's consider
what a link is.

8
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:32,060
In networks,
communication happens

9
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:34,240
over some physical medium.

10
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:37,890
This can be wires or
can happen wirelessly.

11
00:00:37,890 --> 00:00:41,620
The link layer puts some
capabilities on top of that,

12
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:44,140
to not just allow
for transmitting

13
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:46,900
signals over the wires
or through the air,

14
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:50,420
but to enable
communication to occur.

15
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:55,140
This functionality is typically

16
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:59,425
implemented in the network
interface card, or NIC.

17
00:00:59,425 --> 00:01:04,060
The NIC is the component
in your computer which

18
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:06,700
the wire is plugged into or has

19
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:10,325
an antenna to be able
to transmit wirelessly.

20
00:01:10,325 --> 00:01:13,560
Through a combination of
hardware and firmware on

21
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:16,620
the NIC and some functionality
in the operating system,

22
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:20,620
the link layer enables
communication.

23
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:27,000
The first problem that we'll
cover is how to encode data.

24
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,160
We know we can send signals
over a physical medium,

25
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:33,880
but how do you
structure the series of

26
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:36,560
ones and zeros to make

27
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:39,220
it know who the message is

28
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:43,540
destined for and what
the data actually means?

29
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:47,840
The second problem
to address is that

30
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:49,760
the physical medium isn't

31
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:53,760
perfect and these signals
could get corrupted.

32
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:55,940
That is, errors can occur,

33
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:58,080
bits can get flipped.

34
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:02,630
The link layer can allow us
to at least detect that there

35
00:02:02,630 --> 00:02:07,500
was an error and perhaps
even correct the error.

36
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:12,020
Some of the physical mediums

37
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:14,460
that could be used to
communicate are not just

38
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:17,750
point-to-point links
between two devices

39
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:21,290
but may be shared among several.

40
00:02:21,290 --> 00:02:25,405
You could think of a
Wi-Fi network for this.

41
00:02:25,405 --> 00:02:27,760
The key problem to address here

42
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:30,360
is how to regulate access to

43
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:32,280
the physical medium to

44
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:36,180
allow all to be able to
share it effectively.

45
00:02:37,010 --> 00:02:40,140
The last problem
we'll discuss in

46
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:42,440
this module is extending to

47
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:46,200
a local area network that
allows communication to

48
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:51,460
extend beyond the limits of
the transmission medium.

49
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:55,940
Thank you. Next time
we'll get into encoding

50
00:02:55,940 --> 00:03:00,180
the series of ones and
zeros into frames.